{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/gitstore at b697850820b108c2613d61983a48e1f8e18327dd — a plain history question at the storage layer, under ratified proposal c26a7f57 (25981e55), so the thread rail's merge station is derived rather than asserted. Replaces the retired candidate 1a884f46 at 3a45e1f2; the file itself is unchanged by the repair commit, and this artifact republishes with its siblings because the five are reviewed as one set. internal/gitstore/landing.go: Landings reports, for each named commit, whether a branch already carries it and which merge brought it in. Its whole point is that \"absent\" and \"unknown\" are different answers. git merge-base --is-ancestor exits 1 for a commit that is genuinely not an ancestor and 128 for one this clone has never heard of, and collapsing the two turns a command that never ran into a confident negative — the fault that left a false sentence standing in the governing design note for seven days. exitCode therefore treats 0 and 1 as verdicts and anything else as an error carrying git's own words into the reason. A landed commit that arrived by fast-forward has no merge above it and is still reported landed, with its own time, rather than reported absent for want of a merge to name. Commit names reach git's argv, so each is matched against the only shape a commit has before any process starts: no leading dash can become an option and no separator a second argument. LandingLimit bounds the batch here, and as of this head the service enforces that same bound before both of its paths rather than only the one that reaches this function — the review found that the cap was reachable only on success, and the layer that advertises a limit should not depend on its caller taking the happy route. A malformed name is answered rather than dropped, so a caller can always pair request with response, and the layer stays ignorant of application schemas as its architecture row requires. Conditions actually met at this exact head: three tests cover a repository holding a merged branch, an unmerged branch, a commit git has never seen and a name that is not a commit at all — asserting landed with the exact merge hash and a non-zero time, absent, unknown-with-a-reason, and refused-before-git-runs respectively — plus a fast-forwarded commit reported landed with no merge named, and the batch bound. go vet, go test -count=1 ./..., go test -race -count=1 ./..., and the docset gates all pass. The real code path was exercised against this repository: b3bf3083 returns landed with merge 44d8b4fa, and an unmerged head returns absent, so the positive is not a blanket yes. Two commits on main bbc81674; the intervening merges left internal/gitstore untouched, and internal/app's use of the pre-existing HashPayloadTree collides with nothing in this file. Predecessor at this exact path: e9daba2d (#6780) at 889a64a2, to be retired at merge.","body":{"commit":"b697850820b108c2613d61983a48e1f8e18327dd","path":"internal/gitstore"}}